p stays off when the GPIO is floating or during boot. Pulling it up to 3.3 V turns the pump on by default.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"46\\\") (name \\\"/G_{PUMP}\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"R1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") ...) (node (ref \\\"R5\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") ...) (node (ref \\\"T1\\\") (pin \\\"3\\\")...) (node (ref \\\"T2\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"G\\\")...))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"1\\\") (name \\\"+3V3\\\") ... (node (ref \\\"R5\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")) ...)\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R5 /GPUMP +3V3 10k\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R12 0 /GATO 10k\"}], \"impact\": \"At power-up, or whenever the ESP32 GPIO is floating or in reset, the gate is held at 3.3 V and the 24 V main circulation pump switches on unintentionally. This could run dry or flood the reservoir before firmware takes control.\", \"confidence\": 0.4}], \"summary\": \"R28 is entered as 14 \u03a9 where its library part and sibling VREF resistors indicate 14 k\u03a9, which would overdrive the Nutrient-B stepper driver's current reference. R5 pulls the main-pump MOSFET gate up to 3.3 V, unlike the ATO switch it mirrors, which could switch the pump on at boot; this second finding is less certain.\"}",
